{"qid":"Q829356","names":["Makuria","Makuria Kingdom"],"title":"Makuria","endonym":null,"capsule":"Makuria was a Christian Nubian kingdom centered on Dongola, in present-day northern Sudan, attested from the fourth century and dominant in the Middle Nile valley through the medieval period. It repelled an Arab invasion in 651–652, concluding the Baqt treaty — a regulated exchange of goods and non-aggression pact that held for over six centuries — and converted to Chalcedonian Christianity by the mid-sixth century, later aligning with the Coptic patriarchate of Alexandria around 710. The kingdom reached its cultural apex between the ninth and eleventh centuries, producing elaborate wall paintings and ecclesiastical architecture strongly influenced by Byzantine traditions. Internal dynastic conflict fractured the state from 1365 onward, reducing it to a rump polity; the last documented ruler, Joel, appears in records around 1463, after which Funj and Ottoman expansion absorbed its remnant territories in the early sixteenth century.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Makuria's core territory along the Nile from the Third Cataract to the confluence is well-established archaeologically, but lateral extent into the Bayuda Desert and eastern steppes is poorly documented and boundary reconstructions rely heavily on inference.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Makuria","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Makuria"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Makuria","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Makuria"},{"label":"Britannica: Baqt","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Baqt"},{"label":"Britannica: Nubia","url"